            {# ── Slide 2: What Parachute is (three-layer model) ── #}
            <section class="pi-slide fade-up fade-up-3" data-slide-id="2" data-video-start="15" data-video-end="55">
                <p class="pi-label">What Parachute is</p>
                <h2 class="pi-h2">A personal memory and knowledge layer<br>for <em>your</em> data and <em>your</em> AI.</h2>
                <p class="pi-lede">Parachute helps you organize your thoughts, notes, and projects in a coherent way &mdash; without you needing to architect the perfect system first. Just add things in and be in easy conversation with your AI.</p>
                <div class="pi-flywheel">
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Vault</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>The memory + knowledge layer.</strong> Notes, tags, links. Self-hostable. Customizable.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Agent</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>Custom knowledge agents</strong> you can spin up on any vault. Bring the AI you trust to the data you own.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Surface</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>Custom web interfaces</strong> to navigate, view, share, and interface with your data.</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <aside class="pi-notes">
                    <p>Think of Parachute as bringing together the best of what Obsidian offers (notes, tags, links, files on your machine) and what Notion offers (a clean place to organize a project, a life, a team) &mdash; in a truly personal interface.</p>
                    <p>Every vault is reachable over an <strong>open REST API</strong> &mdash; so people can build whatever interface they want on top &mdash; and over <strong>MCP</strong>, so any AI system you use instantly works with your data.</p>
                    <p>The three layers are the conceptual model. Underneath them sit the modules that ship today (vault, scribe, notes, hub, agent) &mdash; we'll get to those in a moment.</p>
                </aside>
            </section>

            {# ── Slide 3: Voice-first origin ── #}
            <section class="pi-slide fade-up fade-up-4" data-slide-id="3" data-video-start="55" data-video-end="80">
                <p class="pi-label">How it started</p>
                <h2 class="pi-h2">A voice note on a walk.</h2>
                <p class="pi-lede">Press a button, talk, let it land. The simplest gesture I could find for staying in conversation with what matters.</p>
                <aside class="pi-notes">
                    <p>Parachute started as a voice journal &mdash; <strong>press a button, talk</strong>, the thought lands. No app to open, no cursor, no prompt window. Capture as you walk, as you wake up, before the meeting starts. Local transcription by default. Audio never leaves the device unless you ask it to.</p>
                    <p>Voice was the gesture, not the whole product. What I kept coming back to was: <em>where do these notes live afterward?</em> Where do they connect? How do they stay yours, and stay useful to whichever AI you're using next? That question is what grew into the rest of Parachute.</p>
                </aside>
            </section>

